{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And I will bless those who bless you, and the one who curses you I will curse.</b> Why does it not say ״וְאָאֹר מְקַלְלֶיךָ״ — 'and I will curse those who curse you' — in the manner that it says ״וַאֲבָרְכָה מְבָרְכֶיךָ״? For ״לְאָדָם מַעַרְכֵי לֵב״ (Proverbs 16:1) — before a person brings forth any utterance from his lips outward, he arranges it first in heart and thought. And it is known that for the good, the Holy One, blessed be He, joins thought to deed; but an evil thought, the Holy One, blessed be He, does not join to deed, as it is written (Psalms 66:18) ״אָוֶן אִם רָאִיתִי בְלִבִּי לֹא יִשְׁמַע ה׳״, and it is written (Isaiah 65:24) ״טֶרֶם יִקְרָאוּ וַאֲנִי אֶעֱנֶה״ etc. For before a person brings forth from his mouth his request and petition, he has already thought in his heart to ask for such and such; therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, answers him from His holy abode before he calls — unlike flesh and blood: when one asks a thing of his fellow, he must express it with his lips, for a person does not know what is in his fellow's heart. But the Holy One, blessed be He, searches hearts and fulfills all the desires of his heart, as it is written (Psalms 20:5) ״יִתֶּן לְךָ כִלְבָבֶךָ״ etc., and as it is said (Proverbs 16:1) ״לְאָדָם מַעַרְכֵי לֵב וּמֵה׳ מַעֲנֵה לָשׁוֹן״. He wished to resolve with this the following difficulty: if indeed the Holy One, blessed be He, fulfills his request after the matter has been arranged in a person's heart, then why did HaShem command prayer by mouth? To this it says ״וּמֵה׳ מַעֲנֵה לָשׁוֹן״ — for this is a gift from HaShem, that He gave an answer of the tongue to man, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yevamot 64a) that the Holy One, blessed be He, longs for the prayer of the righteous, because they are likened before HaShem, blessed be He, to a child dandled before his father. If he asks a thing of his father, even though his father knows what the son needs, nevertheless the father wants the son to express his request from his mouth, because the father delights in the speech of the son's lips. So too the Holy One, blessed be He, says to Israel (Song of Songs 2:14) ״הַשְׁמִיעִינִי אֶת קוֹלֵךְ, כִּי קוֹלֵךְ עָרֵב״ — to draw near to Me. And HaShem, blessed be He, does not wish to deal with us in the manner of princes who do not allow much speech in their presence, and as soon as they grasp the questioner's intent they say to him, 'Shorten your words!' or 'Finish speaking and leave!' But the Holy One, blessed be He, in His kindness is not so, as it is written (Psalms 21:3) ״תַּאֲוַת לִבּוֹ נָתַתָּה לּוֹ״, and nevertheless ״וַאֲרֶשֶׁת שְׂפָתָיו בַּל מָנַעְתָּ סֶּלָה״ — because in His eyes, blessed be He, we are as a child's delight and a precious son. This is what it says: even though ״טֶרֶם יִקְרָאוּ וַאֲנִי אֶעֱנֶה״, nevertheless ״עוֹד הֵם מְדַבְּרִים וַאֲנִי אֶשְׁמָע״, for I have not withheld from them the utterance of their lips." "And this is what is said: ״אָוֶן אִם רָאִיתִי בְלִבִּי לֹא יִשְׁמַע ה׳״ (Psalms 66:18). But lest you say that the same applies also to the good, it teaches: ״אָכֵן שָׁמַע אֱלֹהִים הִקְשִׁיב בְּקוֹל תְּפִלָּתִי״ (Psalms 66:19). For regarding prayer it is not so; rather, He hears the voice of my prayer even though I have not yet brought the utterance forth from my mouth. And if you say, if so, why then does HaShem ask of me ״רְמֹס חֲצֵרָי״ (Isaiah 1:12) — to trample My courts — to pray before Him? To this it says: ״בָּרוּךְ אֱלֹהִים אֲשֶׁר לֹא הֵסִיר תְּפִלָּתִי וְחַסְדּוֹ מֵאִתִּי״ (Psalms 66:20). For I give thanks to His name, blessed be He, that in His kindness He did not remove my prayer and He desires the utterance of my lips, as said above. Therefore it says ״וַאֲבָרְכָה מְבָרְכֶיךָ״ (Genesis 12:3) — for the one who blesses you thinks it in his heart before he brings forth the blessing from his mouth, and a good thought is counted as a deed; therefore I bless him before he brings the blessing forth from his mouth. But regarding those who curse, it is not so; rather, ״וּמְקַלֶּלְךָ״ — after he has brought forth the curse from his mouth, then ״אָאֹר אוֹתוֹ״ — but not before he brings it forth from his mouth, for an evil thought the Holy One, blessed be He, does not join to deed." "Another reason for the matter: for one who pours out from a full and overflowing vessel is not like one who pours out from an empty vessel, in the manner we find with the priestly blessing (Numbers 6:23) ״כֹּה תְבָרְכוּ אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אָמוֹר לָהֶם״, where the prayer leader first says to the priest ״יְבָרֶכְךָ ה׳״ — for the prayer leader is an intermediary between the Holy One, blessed be He, and the priests, and he first says to the priest ״יְבָרֵךְ ה׳״ and brings down upon him the flow of blessing through the channels, so that the priest becomes a vessel full of the blessing of HaShem; and only afterward does the priest pour out from a full vessel upon Israel — for then the blessing takes hold most powerfully when the one who bestows possesses the blessing himself, in essence and primordially. For how can one who is not himself blessed bless? Therefore it says ״וַאֲבָרְכָה מְבָרְכֶיךָ״ — that before they bless you, I will bless him first, so that he becomes a vessel full of the blessing of HaShem and pours upon you from a full and overflowing vessel. But with a curse this consideration does not apply at all; therefore it says ״וּמְקַלֶּלְךָ אָאֹר״ — afterward, and not before. And the fact that it says ״מְבָרְכֶיךָ״ in the plural and ״מְקַלֶּלְךָ״ in the singular, this indicates that many will bless him, but those who curse will not be many — only isolated and contemptible individuals, few and insignificant, like Nimrod and his companions, whose opinion is nullified before everyone." "<b>And the one who curses you I will curse.</b> It should have said ״וּמְקַלֶּלְךָ אֲקַלֵּל״ or ״וְאוֹרֶרְךָ אָאֹר״, and this change is difficult. Also in the verse (I Samuel 2:30) ״כִּי מְכַבְּדַי אֲכַבֵּד וּבֹזַי יֵקָלּוּ״, for it should have said ״וּבֹזַי יִתְבַּזּוּ״. And it appears that these three expressions, each one is harsher than the next, and the order of their rungs is: contempt, curse, execration. For contempt (בִּזָּיוֹן) is the lightest of all, and it is a matter of sitting back and not doing — for the one who shows contempt does not extend honor to the one despised, as it is written (Numbers 15:31) ״כִּי דְבַר ה׳ בָּזָה״. Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Berakhot 8a): this refers to one who sets down a Torah scroll and walks away. And the expression of curse (קְלָלָה) serves also as an expression of contempt, as Rashi explained on the verse ״כִּי קִלְלַת אֱלֹהִים תָּלוּי״ (Deuteronomy 21:23), and it also carries in its meaning an actual curse, close to the expression of execration (אָרוּר). But the expression of execration (אָרוּר) — our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Shevuot 36a): אָרוּר — it includes excommunication, it includes the ban, it includes curse, for this expression includes all of them. And it is known that everything depends on the one who shames and the one who is shamed — for if lowly and base people show contempt for or curse an elder and a man of esteem, it is not sufficient that he himself receive contempt or curse in return, for the pain is not commensurate with the damage: the lowly one does not take his own contempt to heart, and it is as laughter in his eyes. Therefore it could not be said regarding HaShem, blessed be He, ״וּבוֹזַי יִתְבַּזּוּ״, for this is not commensurate with the damage to the King, YHWH of Hosts — that a man who seizes the ropes of contempt (cf. Ketubot 112b, 'the one who holds the ropes of contempt') and despises the word of the King, El exalted and lifted up, YHWH. Therefore HaShem adds upon him curse and disgrace upon his honor, and curses him with a grievous curse commensurate with the one who shows contempt. And likewise Abraham, who was the father of a multitude of nations and before whom all the people of his generation were as nothing — therefore it is not enough that those who curse him receive a curse in return; rather, ״וּמְקַלֶּלְךָ אָאֹר״ — I will add upon them an execration commensurate with their stature. But regarding those who bless and honor, the measure is equal: to bless those who bless him and to honor those who honor him." "And the fact that it says here ״וּמְקַלֶּלְךָ״, while regarding HaShem, blessed be He, it says ״וּבוֹזַי״ — is because the expression of curse does not apply upward, as it were: what could it give and what could it add? And the expression of contempt is more fitting there, for one who does not extend honor as befits the word of HaShem. And flesh and blood ought to be among those who are insulted and not take their contempt to heart, but for a curse one ought to take it to heart. And the fact that it is written here ״וַאֲבָרְכָה מְבָרְכֶיךָ״, while further on (I Samuel 2:30) it says ״כִּי מְכַבְּדַי אֲכַבֵּד״ — the two blessings are not alike, for a blessing to a person is an addition of flow and abundance, and this does not apply upward, as it were, for what could one give Him with his blessing? Rather, its meaning there is a blessing of thanksgiving. And if so, what is the relationship of the blessings to one another such that one would say ״כִּי מְבָרְכַי אֲבָרֵךְ״? And regarding a person, it is not fitting to say ״וַאֲכַבְּדָה מְכַבֶּדְךָ״, for there is no comparison between the honor one person does with his fellow and the honor that the wise inherit from HaShem. But the blessings are alike, for surely it is not in the hand of man to give blessings from his own hand, literally — except that he blesses him, 'May HaShem do good with you,' and with that same blessing with which he blesses him, HaShem blesses also the one who blesses."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {4 "The Aramaic phrase חבלי בוז ובזוזי דבזוזי (Ketubot 112b) is rendered descriptively per the instruction to keep all Hebrew/Aramaic script visible; the parenthetical citation is supplied from the source text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:38.088537185Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}